In this study, nine core variables were defined in alignment with the industrial process and the user’s engineering practice: Material Heating Temperature (MHT) Wedge Stroke (WS) Material Feed Length (MFL) Die Temperature (DT) Product Temperature (PT) Forming Force (FF) – measured as the maximum normalized acoustic amplitude Head Outer Diameter (HOD) Body Outer Diameter (BOD) Product Height (PH) In addition, Left Head Thickness and Right Head Thickness are treated as dimensional dependent variables but are referred to consistently with these names throughout. Among the independent variables, MHT, WS, and MFL were designated as controllable variables, because they can be directly adjusted at the equipment level. The inner diameter of the nut was initially considered as a candidate dependent variable but was excluded from the final analysis due to inspection infeasibility caused by punch-pin fracture during the experiment. The diagrammatic explanation can be seen in the Fig.  1  In this context, controllable variables refer to forming conditions that can be selected and modified by operators on the production equipment and that ultimately allow inference and control of the defect rate of the finished products. MHT denotes the temperature setpoint in the induction heater for the billet. WS represents the magnitude of wedge displacement applied to the die mechanism. This parameter implicitly determines how tightly the die halves are brought into contact and how local pressure is distributed during forming. MFL is the length of material (in mm) supplied into the forming zone to produce a single nut. The remaining variables were measured as process or quality responses: DT is the die surface temperature measured in  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
